Recent analyses of lepton flavor universality (LFU) violating decays have suggested that new physics beyond the Standard Model may exist, potentially allowing us to pinpoint their nature by examining leptoquark theories. However, most research has focused only on certain types of interactions and seldom considered the impact of precise $b 	o c$ data within such models. As a result, we delve into the implications of both $b 	o c$ and $b 	o s$ measurements for the decay $\Lambda_b 	o p 	au  ar{
u}$ within the $U_1$ leptoquark model, and we examine the impact of precise $b 	o c$ data and its implications for predicting $\Lambda_b 	o p 	au  ar{
u}$ decay. Furthermore, we examine the implications of both $b 	o c$ and $b 	o s$ data for the $U_1$ leptoquark model, and we investigate the impact of precise $b 	o c$ data in predicting $\Lambda_b 	o p 	au  ar{
u}$ decay. Relying on a light-cone sum rule, we calculate the form factors that are essential for the decay $\Lambda_b 	o p 	au  ar{
u}$, and we derive the branching ratio and relevant observables, and finally, we illustrate our findings through representative plots.

Using 3.2 inverse femtobarns of LHCb data, the form factor $f_{+}(q^{2})$ is directly measured for the $B_{s}^{0}ightarrow D_{s}^{*-}\mu^{+}
u_{\mu}$ decay, after accounting for the small impact of $D_{s}^{-*}$ spin. By parameterizing the $B_{s}^{0}ightarrow D_{s}^{-*}\mu^{+}
u_{\mu}$ decay with a Beta function and $D_{s}^{-*}$ decay using an angular density, both independent of form factors, we derive $f_{+}(q^{2})$ from the $B_{s}^{0}$ decay data and compare the results with those from $B^{0}ightarrow D^{*-}\ell^{+}
u_{\ell}$ decays. Additionally, by utilizing the known $D_{s}^{-}ightarrow K^{-}K^{+}\pi^{-}$ decay branching fraction and theoretical $D_{s}^{-}ightarrow (K^{-}K^{+}\pi^{-})$ decay fractions, we utilize approximately 25% of the $D_{s}^{-}$ decays to further refine the form factor measurement. This additional constraint allows us to reduce the associated uncertainty by a comparable amount.
